nodecode-writing-guard is a rewrite of MadeByBrain's MadeByBrain/hermes-extensions:

  https://github.com/MadeByBrain/hermes-extensions
  commit c18ef190635e386a627ba921c816dceeb28c2de8
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 MadeByBrain (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin is three files of pure rules (guard.py,
phrases.py) and one registration (__init__.py) that hooked the harness's
transform_llm_output to append one italic report line under every answer.
The rewrite keeps the rules and the report: the dash table, the ordered
replacement that keeps a range and a bullet and turns a spoken dash into a
comma or a spaced hyphen, the masking of fenced code, inline code, URLs,
markdown link targets, MEDIA and quoted lines, the language guess by word
count, the du/Sie counting that sets aside "du/Sie" and quoted words, the
mixing-in-one-paragraph rule and the footer's wording in both languages.
The phrase table is the original's, all of it, with the same ids. The seam
moved: the checks now advise NLE::EMIT-STREAM-PART, so a fragment is fixed
where it is made and the line rides the answer itself in every shell,
channel and transcript, and the plugin's check-draft skill became one
standing paragraph in the prompt and the writing_guard_check tool.

What was left out, and why: the du/Sie switch store (a JSON file under the
harness's home, keyed by session, with a file lock, a 30-day retention and
a 2000-session cap) is not reproduced -- a Nodecode cell reads the answer
in front of it, and /writing-guard names the switch in its last report
instead of remembering one per chat across processes. The platform filter
(disabled_platforms: cron, subagent, kanban, batch, webhook) has no
equivalent, because the advice sits on the stream every lane shares. The
slash command's argument moved: /writing-guard TEXT checks a text,
/writing-guard on|off|reset controls it, and the original's "check" word is
still accepted before the text. A dash at the very start of a streamed
fragment is read as a list bullet, which is what a whole-answer rewrite
would not do.

What the rewrite adds: settings for what the original hard-coded (the line's
language, whether the primer rides the prompt), the extra_phrases and
ignore_phrases the original had, the primer setting, and the counters
/writing-guard reports.
